{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Let there be a firmament.</b> In Ḥagigah (12a) Rabbi Yoḥanan said in the name of Rav: At the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, created the world, it was expanding and going like two shuttles of a weaver's warp, until the Holy One, blessed be He, rebuked it and made it stand, as it says (Job 26:11): ״עַמּוּדֵי שָׁמַיִם יְרוֹפָפוּ וְיִתְמְהוּ מִגַּעֲרָתוֹ״. And that is what Reish Lakish said: What is written (Genesis 35:11): ״אֲנִי אֵל שַׁדַּי״? I am the One who said to My world: 'Enough (dai).' And some say: At the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, created the sea, it was expanding and going until the Holy One, blessed be He, rebuked it, etc. Now one must ponder what the author of this statement has informed us here — for what was, was. And as it appears, he came to speak on behalf of man: that which we see, namely, that by his nature he always craves that his deeds spread forth without end, without boundary, and that everything be permitted in his eyes — whatever he desires he will do. And if man were left to his nature, then there would be no limit or boundary to the mounts of the object of his desire, and in lawlessness he would be content not to be bounded and tied by any practice at all — until HaShem rebuked us by means of this Torah, which gives boundary and measure to all deeds: how far they may extend according to the divine will, and how far he is permitted to loosen the reins of his desire." "One may wonder about man: how did he not find in his intellect to set for himself a boundary and measure in all his deeds, such as the intellectually understood commandments and their like, without divine admonition — since reason itself demands it? About this he said: Do not marvel at the desire, for man received the nature of his progenitors. For every man is fashioned from heaven, earth, and sea: the soul from the heavens, and the body from the earth, which was made like clay by means of the waters, as Rashi explained on the verse (Genesis 2:6): ״וְאֵד יַעֲלֶה מִן הָאָרֶץ״. And from the three of them a complete man (אִישׁ) was made, for אִישׁ is an acrostic of אֶרֶץ, יָם, שָׁמַיִם. And for the most part the nature of the progenitor is found in the offspring, and man too received the nature of his progenitors — heaven, earth, and sea — for all of them were expanding and going without end until HaShem rebuked them. For what was said, 'it was going and expanding,' may be said also of the earth, for this reason he mentioned two shuttles, and from then onward an established nature was fixed in everything fashioned from them: like them, their makers would act — that they have a desire to spread forth in all their deeds without end." "And corresponding to this, the author of the statement came to reprove man, saying: If he received their nature to have a craving to spread forth as mentioned, behold, at the least he should receive from them also the good quality of standing firm at the rebuke of HaShem, blessed be He — just as they stand at the rebuke of YHWH with a measure from which they do not depart by as much as a hair's breadth — so should man stand at the rebuke of HaShem and at the measure and limitation that HaShem has set for us in all deeds by means of this Torah. And this is the intent of the statement in Yalkut, parashat Ha'azinu (Deuteronomy 32:982): ״Look at the heavens and the earth and the sea, etc. — have they perhaps changed their measure?״ etc. And this is the reason our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 17a) regarding the commandment of tsitsit: tekhelet resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the firmament, etc. For by means of the tekhelet one remembers the sea and the firmament and learns from them to stand at the rebuke of HaShem, not to alter the measure, as will be explained with the help of HaShem (Numbers 15:38). And as for why it was not said 'let there be a firmament and there was a firmament,' in the manner that 'and there was light' was said — this is because an evil decree is not made swiftly; therefore the firmament, which alludes to division, was not made immediately, whereas light, which is good for everything, was made swiftly — therefore it says immediately ״וַיְהִי אוֹר״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "The Yalkut citation 'דברים לב:תתקמב' uses the Hebrew numeral system (תתקמב = 982) for the remez index in Yalkut Shim'oni on Ha'azinu, not a verse number in Deuteronomy 32; retained as the address the author cited."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:32:44.106054906Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}